<Project>

  <!--
    The single source of truth for the agent's version.

    Careful: the version used to live in three places with nothing tying them together:
      - Program.cs: `const string Version = "0.1.0"`  (what the server was told)
      - installer/build.ps1: `$Version = '0.1.0'`      (what went into the MSI)
      - no `<Version>` anywhere, so the assembly was 1.0.0.0

    The result was silent but serious: after building MSI 0.2.0 and rolling it out to
    15 PCs, the agent still reported itself as 0.1.0 in its heartbeat. The server used
    that stale value to decide whether to offer an update, so machines that had already
    updated were offered the same update again and again, and the staged rollout never
    finished.

    Now a single change here reaches all three places:
      - the assembly's InformationalVersion  (Program.cs reads it at runtime)
      - the MSI's ProductVersion             (build.ps1 reads it from here)
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      Version history. The rule behind every bump: raise the number BEFORE a binary is
      handed out by hand, not after the code changes. Two different binaries with the
      same name and version cannot be told apart, and signing changes the bytes too.

      0.1.0 -> 0.2.0: four real agent changes: capture stops when the device is revoked,
      the queue's disk budget is actually enforced, `logs\agent.log`, and the config
      loop. Without the bump no update could be rolled out: `rollout.ts` compares
      SemVer, so a PC running 0.1.0 would not be offered 0.1.0. The MSI itself is
      installed with `AllowSameVersionUpgrades="yes"`, but that only helps when
      installing by hand, and the whole staged-rollout mechanism would have stalled.

      0.2.0 -> 0.3.0: new code (sign-in window, enroll-login, certificate pinning), but
      the real reason for the bump was different: three different binaries had gone out
      under the name 0.2.0 (without the sign-in window, with it, and with pinning). The
      file name and version were identical, so there was no way to tell which was which,
      and the owner ran an old one and thought the feature was missing.

      0.3.0 -> 0.3.1: a bug found AFTER 0.3.0 was handed to the owner (the log had no
      UTF-8 BOM, so Get-Content showed garbled characters).

      0.3.1 -> 0.3.2: the change was in the installer, not the agent, but the number is
      tied to the agent. The installer created the logon task but did not start it, and
      the task's trigger is logon, so the installer reported success and nothing ran
      until the next reboot. The binary itself was fine: running it by hand showed the
      sign-in window.

      0.3.2 -> 0.3.3: no code change. 0.3.2 was built wrongly, without `-ServerUrl`, so
      the address was not baked into the MSI and double-clicking it stopped with "This
      MSI was built without a server address". The bump keeps two different MSIs from
      circulating under one name; the default in build.ps1 was changed so the mistake
      cannot happen again.

      0.3.3 -> 0.3.4: the owner noticed a green "Working" in the tray without being
      signed in, and 7 rows piled up in the outbox. Not just a display error: the time
      before sign-in stayed in the outbox, and once staff signed in the device was bound
      to them and that time went into their record, e.g. half an hour of admin work
      counted as someone else's attendance.

      0.3.4 -> 0.3.5: the previous fix worked ("Not signed in", QUEUED 0), but raised a
      new question: there was no option to sign in. True: the window appeared only once,
      at startup. After closing it the only way back was logging off and on again, while
      the screen said "Sign in to start counting your hours". The task was announced but
      there was no door to do it.

      0.3.5 -> 0.3.6: no code change. The MSI and both exes are now signed (ADR-014).

      0.3.6 -> 0.3.7: no code change. The server address baked into the MSI changed from
      the office LAN to the VPS. 0.3.6 had gone to the owner with the old address.

      0.3.8 -> 0.4.1: behaviour changed, so the minor version went up:
        - 0.4.0: time counting stops when the screen has not changed at all for 10 minutes
        - 0.4.1: tray item "Install update...", so staff can install a new version themselves

      Careful: this number MUST match `build.ps1 -Version`. The build warns when they
      differ. Without the match the MSI would say 0.4.1 while the agent told the server
      0.3.8, so the update check would offer the same version forever and the dashboard
      would show the wrong version. Exactly this mismatch was caught while building 0.4.1.

      0.4.10: built but deliberately NOT published at the time. It bundles two changes:
      seeing the app even when idle, and measuring the second half of the hero number.
      `UpdateService.offerFor` offers only the newest non-halted version, so publishing
      0.4.10 would have halted the running `partial` rollout of 0.4.9, whose shutdown
      goodbye fix was still being measured. Publishing is the owner's decision (Settings
      -> Agent updates -> Publish). The number was still raised right away: otherwise an
      MSI built from HEAD would also say "0.4.9" with different code inside, and the
      update logic decides who needs what by version name alone. This number must match
      `build.ps1 -Version` here too (see the note above).

      0.4.11: sends the time-bounded goodbye event on `WM_ENDSESSION` (`TryFlushGoodbye`,
      2 s total ceiling). Raised right away for the same reason as 0.4.10. Publishing and
      rollout are separate decisions, and they belong to the owner (Settings -> Agent
      updates). The shutdown-goodbye work is only closed once the goodbye delay has been
      measured a fourth time after the rollout and has dropped to seconds: green CI is
      not the same as a real PC shutting down.

      0.4.12 (fork, 5 Oct 2026): build.ps1 -HideLatestShot. Raised so a build
      made from here is not mistaken for the 0.4.11 already installed, and so
      Settings → Agent updates can offer it (it compares version names).
      0.5.0 (fork, 6 Oct 2026): daylight-saving time zones (the server's table of
      offset changes, `zoneTransitions`) and work policies with no hours target
      (the tray shows hours only instead of hanging on "Loading…").
    -->
    <Version>0.5.0</Version>

    <!--
      AssemblyVersion keeps only three numbers (no SemVer pre-release part): the Windows
      version resource does not accept `1.2.3-beta`, and trying to set it breaks the build.
    -->
    <AssemblyVersion>$(Version).0</AssemblyVersion>
    <FileVersion>$(Version).0</FileVersion>

    <Company>oXeio</Company>
    <Product>oXeio Monitor</Product>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
